From c353dca3564b65d4537740710321b46943ae6f52 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Youll <andrewyoull86@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:34:57 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] avformat producer: preferred decoders, falling back to
 the default, and drop_before after a jump

The prefer property (or MLT_AVFORMAT_PREFER, or a default chosen at build
time with MLT_AVFORMAT_PREFER_DEFAULT) lists decoders to try first, as
codec=decoder pairs: on RISC OS, h264=h264_vchiq,hevc=hevc_hwdec, the
Raspberry Pi's hardware decoders. Such a decoder refuses streams it
can't take. When it fails to open, the default decoder is opened
instead; when it fails part way (ENOSYS or AVERROR_EXTERNAL), the
default decoder is opened and the producer seeks again to where it was.
meta.media.decoder names the decoder in use.

After a seek, or a jump forward that is decoded through, a preferred
decoder is given drop_before (an option of
the RISC OS hardware decoders; others ignore it), so the pictures before
the one asked for are left out instead of handed over to be thrown away.

A preferred decoder is opened only when the clip's first picture is
decoded, not with the clip: opening a project opens every clip, and on
the Raspberry Pi every clip's hardware decoder then held VideoCore memory
before any was used, so the hardware encoder couldn't start an export.

MLT_AVFORMAT_PREFER_MAX (or MLT_AVFORMAT_PREFER_MAX_DEFAULT at build
time) is how many of each preferred decoder may be open at once, across
all producers. On a Pi 4 with gpu_mem=128 it must be 1: a second
h264_vchiq decoder opens but never gives a picture, and four or more can
stop the VideoCore answering. A clip that needs a place while all are
taken uses the default decoder; once the holder has gone
MLT_AVFORMAT_PREFER_IDLE pictures (default 5, counted across producers)
without decoding, the clip takes its place over: the holder's decoder is
closed first, then the clip's opened and decoding starts again from a
seek. Two clips both in use, as in a dissolve, keep what they have.
Places are given back when a decoder is closed or falls back, and any
still open at exit are closed (on RISC OS nothing else would close them).
